WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
1. Select the native Basic case
Choose Cloud → Basic → ISSM → SquareIceShelf.
Start with your verified AWS account, configured MATLAB license and any required institutional Connector.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:28.000
2. Understand the synthetic shelf
The supplied case is a 1,000 km square floating shelf. Thickness tapers from 1,000 m to 300 m along y.
It solves one steady SSA stress-balance problem.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:44.000
3. Keep the canonical defaults
Basic overrides are disabled for this reference run.
The script requests 100 km nominal mesh spacing and 2 MPI processes. The resulting mesh has 91 vertices and 148 elements.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:58.000
4. Review the model and image
Review ISSM, SquareIceShelf and the tested image/digest.
This actual run used 2 vCPU, 8 GiB and a time limit of 60 minutes. Verify the target account and licensing readiness before launch.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:12.000
5. Launch the reviewed run
Confirm the readiness rows, including ISSM runtime. Select Launch cloud run.
CryoLauncher stages the reviewed inputs and submits the native job.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:28.000
6. Let the native job run
Actual timings from this accepted AWS job:
Submit to container start:214.4 s.
Batch start to stop:42.8 s.
The 283.7 s recording pause also includes retrieval and observation time.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Native MATLAB / ISSM run completed
The actual AWS Batch run succeeded with native exit code 0.
Downloaded runtime provenance reports MATLAB R2024b Update 7 and ISSM 2026.1. This confirms that the configured license path supported actual execution.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:56.000
7. Select the structured field
Open Results, select StressbalanceSolution and Vel, then Render.
The current package has 91 nodal speed values and four native physical fields, plus a convergence diagnostic.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:12.000
8. Interpret the full saved Vel map
This full figure comes from this run’s browser-downloaded result ZIP.
Vel is horizontal speed in m/year. The 91 mesh vertices span approximately 0 to 9,034 m/year. Axes are in metres; 10⁶ m equals 1,000 km.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:26.000
9. Save the actual results
Choose Download results, then the generated HTTPS archive link.
The browser saved this run’s ZIP successfully: 11 members and valid CRC. It includes fields, mesh, native model, figure and provenance.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:40.000
10. Check the accepted evidence
All four physical fields and the mesh match the declared reference exactly. The native convergence count is 23 steps.
Keep the source packet, run provenance and downloaded outputs together.
